  "summary": {
    "en": "Past a certain angle, light trying to leave glass is reflected back completely."
  },
  "cards": [
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Leaving the glass"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Here light travels inside a semicircular glass block and meets the flat face. Going from glass into air it bends away from the normal. A weak ray is also reflected back inside."
      },
      "equation": null,
      "modelState": {
        "angle": 25,
        "n": 1.5,
        "showNormal": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "The critical angle"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Increase the angle of incidence. The refracted ray swings down towards the surface. At the critical angle c it runs along the boundary, at 90° to the normal."
      },
      "equation": "\\sin c = \\dfrac{1}{n}",
      "modelState": {
        "angle": 41,
        "n": 1.5,
        "showNormal": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Total internal reflection"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Go past the critical angle and no light escapes. All of it is reflected inside the glass. This only happens when light is in the denser material heading for a less dense one."
      },
      "equation": null,
      "modelState": {
        "angle": 55,
        "n": 1.5,
        "showNormal": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Why it matters"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Optical fibres trap light by total internal reflection, carrying signals around bends with almost no loss. A material with a larger n has a smaller critical angle, which is part of why diamonds sparkle."
      },
      "equation": null,
      "modelState": {
        "angle": 30,
        "n": 2.4,
        "showNormal": true
      }
    }
